# The Bluetooth switch comes up BLOCKED on every boot of every card.
#
# This is a THIRD rules file, separate from both 99-dashberry.rules (device
# symlinks) and 99-dashberry-rfkill.rules (the wlan block), and the split is
# the point: firstinstall REMOVES the wlan rules file on the one build meant
# to boot live (a debug card given a --wifi profile). Bluetooth has no such
# build. Nothing in DashBerry speaks BT, no card is ever meant to, and there
# is no session — nothing like JOIN WIFI — that could ever want it up. Put
# this rule in that file and the one card allowed wlan would silently be
# allowed BT too; here, no code path on any card removes it.
#
# Depth, not the whole defense — the same lesson the wlan rule learned the
# hard way (2026-08-05, 2026-08-08). Current Pi OS images ship
# rfkill_default.conf (`options rfkill default_state=0`), so the kernel
# already registers every switch soft-blocked and this write is usually
# 1-over-1; on an older image, or if that conf goes away, this is what
# blocks the switch as it appears. Either way an add-time state is only the
# FIRST word of a boot: bluetoothd's AutoEnable (default true since BlueZ
# 5.51) un-blocks the adapter and powers it seconds later, and on a writable
# OS systemd-rfkill restores its own saved state over the top. What actually
# holds a boot BT-KILLED is dashberry-btkill.service, ordered after
# bluetooth.service so it speaks last, over an adapter that dtoverlay=disable-bt
# means should not exist at all; firstinstall masking bluetooth.service and
# hciuart.service sits between them.
#
# ACTION=="add" is load-bearing here for the same reason as in the wlan
# rule: an rfkill state change also reaches udev, and an unqualified rule
# would re-write the attribute inside the kernel's own change event. Only
# the switch APPEARING — boot, or a driver rebind — forces it down; holding
# it down afterwards is the service's job, not udev's.
ACTION=="add", SUBSYSTEM=="rfkill", ATTR{type}=="bluetooth", ATTR{soft}="1"
